## 15. Execution strategy

Work in the following order.

### Phase A — Inspect and establish a baseline

Read the specifications, inspect all source files and ZIP contents, run existing tests, identify the actual production implementation, and record verified findings.

### Phase B — Protect user data

Fix transactional restore, transactional reset, data validation, storage failures, recovery behavior, and unsafe rendering.

Do not move on while the data-integrity implementation is obviously unsafe.

### Phase C — Complete core functionality

Fix nested subtasks, missing transaction editing, currency handling, calendar boundaries, milestone confirmations, and other verified gaps against the original specification.

### Phase D — Establish the canonical build

Resolve duplicate app implementations, remove unrelated starter-project requirements, create a deterministic production build, and ensure the output remains self-contained.

### Phase E — Accessibility and UX

Improve contrast, dialog focus behavior, keyboard support, status messaging, mobile layout, and reduced-motion support while maintaining the existing product personality.

### Phase F — Test and verify

Run the full automated suite. Test the actual generated artifact. Perform available browser, accessibility, offline, persistence, import, and export checks.

Fix failures discovered during testing.

### Phase G — Document and package

Write the accurate README, maintain the license, update `.gitignore`, create a clean release ZIP, and verify its contents.

Do not stop after producing a plan or a first working-looking screen. Continue until the available implementation and verification work is complete.

